<h1>Exercise Name: Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Row (Wide-Grip)</h1>
<u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Rows (Wide-Grip) viewed from
     a rear quarter angle. The video highlights the upper and middle back muscles—specifically the trapezius,
     rhomboids, rear deltoids, and lats (Trapezius, Rhomboids, Posterior Deltoids, Latissimus Dorsi)—in glowing
     yellow-orange colors, illustrating how pulling a cable bar toward the lower chest in a bent-over stance
      engages the back muscles.
<b>Exercise Name:</b> Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Row(Wide-Grip)<br>

Target Muscles:Middle Back (Rhomboids, Middle & Lower Trapezius), Rear Deltoids (Posterior Deltoids),
  and Upper Lats (Latissimus Dorsi)
<b>Secondary Muscles:</b> Erector Spinae (lower back stability), Biceps, Forearms, Glutes & Hamstrings
  (isometrically held)
</div></div>

<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1>
<u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>
Attach a straight or wide bar to the lowest pulley of a cable machine. Stand facing the machine, hinge forward at your hips while keeping your back straight and knees slightly bent (roughly 45-degree angle torso slant). Grab the bar with an overhand grip wider than shoulder-width.
<u><b><h4>2. Pulling Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>
Exhale as you pull the cable bar upward toward your lower chest / upper abdomen. Keep your elbows flared slightly outward to shift emphasis onto the middle back and rear shoulders.
<u><b><h4>3. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>
Pause for a split second at the top when the bar reaches your torso, squeezing your shoulder blades together.
<u><b><h4>4. Return Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>
Inhale as you slowly extend your arms back down toward the pulley under control until you feel a full stretch across your middle back.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Constant Cable Tension:</b> Unlike barbell rows where tension fluctuates, cables maintain uninterrupted resistance throughout both the stretch and squeeze phases.<br>
 
 <b>◉Upper & Middle Back Thickness:</b> Wide-grip execution isolates the rhomboids, rear delts, and middle traps to build upper back depth and density.<br>
 
 <b>◉Posterior Chain Engagement:</b> Maintaining the bent-over hip hinge position builds lower back isometric endurance and core stability.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 minutes |

Keep your spine straight and core braced throughout every rep—avoid rounding your lower back or bouncing your torso up and down to swing the weight.

